      "thinking": "I'm tracing the monthly-spend-feature flag through use_monthly_statement into render_monthly_statement, since the p99 latency spike suggests slow paths for accounts with long histories. I notice purchases_this_month(account) gets called multiple times in that render function, which looks like a likely source of redundant, expensive computation.\n\nThe band and category summaries each iterate a fixed number of buckets against the purchase list, giving linear overhead, but that alone should raise p50 too, not just p99 \u2014 so the real issue is probably that heavy accounts with large purchase histories are walking the full history repeatedly rather than just this month's data, which would explain why only the tail is affected.\n\nThen I spot it: `average_spend_per_item` in spend_summary is doing a nested loop, recomputing a running sum of all earlier purchase prices for every purchase index. That's the quadratic culprit \u2014 for accounts with many purchases, this blows up exactly the way the p99 spike suggests.\n\nThere's a similar pattern in `typical_spend_per_item`, using repeated remove(min(remaining)) on a list, which is also O(n\u00b2) \u2014 but that one's gated behind the \"typical spend\" rollout flag, distinct from monthly-spend-feature. Since the target_app repo mapping flag names to parameters isn't available, the only clue linking monthly-spend-feature to the statement path is the docstring in monthly_statement.py itself, which points to the average_spend_per_item calculation as the likely fault.\n\nChecking the rest of the render path \u2014 purchases_this_month, band and category summaries, the_usual_month \u2014 they're all linear, just with a moderate constant factor from being called multiple times. That's consistent with p50 staying flat while p99 spikes for accounts with unusually large purchase histories, since the quadratic function would dominate only for those large-n accounts.\n\nBut maybe I'm missing something deeper \u2014 perhaps main has changed which function the statement calls, or there's a flag gating typical_spend_per_item that behaves differently than documented. I should check the test file and pricing/visits code for a hidden quadratic path rather than assuming it's just a big constant.\n\nActually the docstring practically spells it out: \"the cost follows the shape of the code rather than anything it is waiting on\" \u2014 a clear signal of an algorithmic complexity bug, not I/O latency. typical_spend_per_item likely repeatedly scans and removes the minimum from a list each iteration, giving roughly O(n\u00b2) behavior \u2014 for a couple thousand purchases that's millions of operations, enough to explain a slow p99 while smaller histories stay fast. The test file even seems to hint that this cost is deliberately modeled rather than asserted directly, reinforcing that this is the intended bottleneck to find.\n\nActually if the flag were off, average_spend_per_item (quadratic) still renders, so off would be slower \u2014 meaning monthly-spend-feature isn't use_monthly_summary after all. Given the explicit docstring naming that flag for the statement path, I should trust that match and focus on finding the superlinear cost within the monthly statement rendering itself, checking whether something like purchases_this_month has a hidden quadratic pattern.\n\nI'm re-examining render_monthly_statement: it recomputes purchases_this_month five separate times for bought, biggest, smallest, mean, and usual month \u2014 but that's still linear overall, so five linear passes shouldn't cause a 6-10x jump against an already-quadratic lifetime average baseline. There must be something else the statement path triggers that I'm missing.\n\nI should check test_monthly_statement.py for scale hints, and also look at visits.py and pricing_service.py \u2014 maybe record_visit is called on every page load, which isn't flag-gated but could still be the real hot path worth checking.\n\n",
